---
/**
 * The privacy page's prose, in English. See `./index.ts` for why the facts arrive as props.
 */
import type { PrivacyProseProps } from './index';

const { section, measurementId, retentionMonths, consentKey, email, booking, name } =
  Astro.props as PrivacyProseProps;
---

{section === 'site' && (
  <>
    <p>
      The pages are plain HTML and CSS, built ahead of time and served as files by GitHub Pages.
      There is no server of mine behind them, no database, and no form that sends anything anywhere.
      The site itself sets no cookie.
    </p>
    <p>
      GitHub serves those files, so GitHub sees each request — the address it came from, the browser
      that made it, the URL it asked for — and keeps it in its own logs, under its own privacy
      statement. Those logs are not available to me: a GitHub Pages site gives its author no access
      log at all, which is the reason the measurement below exists.
    </p>
  </>
)}

{section === 'measurement' && (
  <>
    <p>
      With your consent, and only then, the page loads Google Analytics 4 — property{' '}
      <code>{measurementId}</code>. Until you allow it your browser makes no request to Google at
      all: the loader is not in the page waiting to run, it is added by the script that records your
      answer.
    </p>
    <p>
      What is recorded is the page you are reading, the page that referred you, an approximate
      location derived from your address, and your device, browser and language. The address itself
      is used to derive that location and is not stored in the property. Two first-party cookies,{' '}
      <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_…</code>, are set in your browser so that a second visit is not
      counted as a first one. While measurement is allowed, clicks on links that leave the site and
      downloads of the CV are recorded as events.
    </p>
    <p>
      Advertising is refused in the same call that starts measurement: <code>ad_storage</code>,{' '}
      <code>ad_user_data</code> and <code>ad_personalization</code> are set to denied and are never
      updated, so there is no advertising cookie, no remarketing audience, and nothing with which to
      personalise an advert. Google processes what is collected on my behalf under its own terms,
      and the property keeps it for {retentionMonths} months, after which Google deletes the records
      at the level of an individual visitor.
    </p>
  </>
)}

{section === 'choice' && (
  <>
    <p>
      Your answer is kept as a single entry in your browser's local storage —{' '}
      <code>{consentKey}</code>, holding <code>granted</code> or <code>denied</code>. It is not a
      cookie, it is never sent anywhere, and its only purpose is that the notice does not ask twice.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can change it here at any time. Withdrawing deletes the <code>_ga</code> cookies this site
      set and reloads the page without the measurement script. Clearing site data in your browser
      forgets the answer too, and the notice will ask again.
    </p>
  </>
)}

{section === 'elsewhere' && (
  <>
    <p>
      The email address is a <code>mailto:</code> link: it opens your own mail client, and what you
      write arrives in my mailbox. The booking link goes to{' '}
      <a href={booking.href}>{booking.label}</a>, a separate service with its own privacy policy —
      nothing about you reaches it until you follow the link. The links to GitHub, LinkedIn and the
      articles published elsewhere are ordinary links, and each destination decides its own
      handling.
    </p>
    <p>
      The CV is a PDF served from this site, so downloading it involves no third party beyond the
      request itself.
    </p>
  </>
)}

{section === 'contact' && (
  <>
    <p>
      This site is published by {name} in Tokyo, Japan. For anything about this page, or about data
      it may hold on you, write to <a href={`mailto:${email}`}>{email}</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you are in the European Union or the United Kingdom: you may ask what is held about you,
      ask for it to be deleted, or object to the measurement — and withdrawing consent above is the
      direct way to do the last of those. The only data this site puts anywhere is the measurement
      described above and whatever you choose to send by email.
    </p>
  </>
)}
